    "body": "Honda will build a new $3 billion auto plant in Central Ohio to meet growing demand for hybrids in North America, the company announced on Monday.\nWhat’s Related\nThe plant will add more than 2,000 jobs and can build up to 240,000 vehicles a year. Production is expected to start in 2029, pending final government approvals. Once it’s running, Honda’s U.S. auto production capacity will grow to about 1.3 million vehicles a year.\nThe plant will sit on land between Honda’s East Liberty and Marysville auto plants. It will be Honda’s fourth auto plant in Ohio and its ninth auto-related production facility in the U.S. Honda said it can build hybrid, gasoline, and battery-electric vehicles .\n“We are making one of the most significant commitments in our history to position Honda for the next 50 years,” said Bob Nelson, executive vice president of American Honda Motor Co.\nNelson added that the investments make the U.S. a key part of Honda’s global future.\nRELATED STORIES: Honda Expands Ohio Manufacturing With $1 Billion Investment | Honda Launches Electric Cargo Vehicle for Urban Delivery\nWhy Ohio?\nHonda pointed to the state’s skilled workforce, its large base of nearby suppliers, its existing plants and development centers, and the L-H Battery Company joint venture. Honda has also put $132 million into research facilities and endowed chairs at Ohio State University over four decades.\nOhio Gov. Mike DeWine noted that Honda and Ohio have grown together for nearly 50 years. He said the company picked Ohio because of the many advantages the state offers.\nUpgrades in Ohio, Alabama, and Georgia\nThe new plant is part of a bigger round of investments in Ohio, Alabama, and Georgia to build more hybrid vehicles, engines, and hybrid systems in the U.S.\nHonda will retool its Transmission Plant-Ohio to build two next-generation hybrid systems. That will raise hybrid system capacity in Ohio by 250 percent in the coming years. The plant already builds the two-motor hybrid system for the CR-V, Accord, and Civic.\nNearby, the Anna Engine Plant, Honda’s largest engine plant in the world, is retooling a line for a next-generation four-cylinder engine for future compact and midsize vehicles. A new parts consolidation center there is expected to open next year.\nAt its Alabama Auto Plant, Honda is expanding engine production for a new V6 hybrid engine and renovating Line 1 to build the Passport. In Georgia, Honda’s transmission plant will add a new assembly line to make hybrid systems for larger vehicles.\nHonda is also retooling its plant in Alliston, Ontario, for next-generation hybrid and gasoline models, and it’s bringing engine production back there.\nThe moves follow Honda’s May 2026 announcement that it plans to launch 15 new Honda and Acura hybrid models globally by 2030. About 99 percent of the Honda and Acura vehicles sold in the U.S. in 2025 were built in North America.",
